Bring-up gate: stop first boot from eating the vendor fallback.

Unmodified HMCLOCK-EN, on any OTP-chain boot where slot 0 differs
(magic/size/version), runs selflash_install() and overwrites slot 0 with
the running image. On this BWR tag slot 0 holds the vendor bank, so
the first boot of a stock build destroys the only BLE-accessible fallback.

This patch gates that call behind BRINGUP_NO_SELFFLASH. Apply to the
HMCLOCK-EN (or HMCLOCK) tree at build-staging time, define the symbol for
bring-up builds only, and REMOVE it (rebuild without the define) once
panel + BLE + OTA are proven — persistence across power cycles for the
RAM-run path depends on selflash.

With the gate on:
- Vendor-OTA-delivered image boots from the inactive slot, vendor bank in
  slot 0 untouched, bootloader keeps booting the new image (newest valid).
- All later updates go through HMCLOCK's own OTA (0xFF00 service, its web
  app, device name DCLK-xxxx). BLE flashing is never interrupted.
- Rollback = OTA the vendor .bin back through the HMCLOCK web app
  (same file magic; u16 size + version reads tolerate it), or SWD restore
  from the dump. Rollback path is UNTESTED — dump stays mandatory.

Keil: add BRINGUP_NO_SELFFLASH under Project -> Options -> C/C++ -> Define.
GCC/e2studio: append -DBRINGUP_NO_SELFFLASH to CFLAGS for the bring-up build.

--- a/src/epd/spi_flash.c
+++ b/src/epd/spi_flash.c
@@ -590,7 +590,11 @@
 		printk("Slots: %08x + %08x\n", image_addr[0], image_addr[1]);
 
+#ifdef BRINGUP_NO_SELFFLASH
+		printk("bring-up: selflash_install suppressed, slot 0 untouched\n");
+#else
 		selflash_install(firm_size, image_addr[0], image_addr[1]);
+#endif
 
 		fspi_exit();
 		return 0;
